  "verbalization": "Rubiyunnanin F is shown to have a target relationship with SNU-387, which is a compound that acts as a target of multiple molecular entities, including 10,22-Dihydroxy-7,7,18,18-tetramethyl-8,13,17-trioxapentacyclo[12.8.0.03,12.04,9.016,21]docosa-1(22),3(12),4(9),10,14,16(21),19-heptaen-2-one, (2R,3R,4S,5S,6R)-2-[(1S)-4-[(3R)-3-hydroxybutyl]-3,5,5-trimethylcyclohex-3-en-1-yl]oxy-6-[[(2S,3R,4S,5S)-3,4,5-trihydroxyoxan-2-yl]oxymethyl]oxane-3,4,5-triol, and 3-Bromo-4-(2,3-Dibromo-4,5-Dihydroxybenzyl)-5-Methoxymethylpyrocatecol. All of these molecules are implicated in the inhibition of Phosphodiesterase 6C, a key enzyme involved in the regulation of intracellular signaling and cellular homeostasis. The evidence suggests that Rubiyunnanin F may act through a common mechanism, targeting SNU-387, which in turn inhibits Phosphodiesterase 6C. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role for Rubiyunnanin F in modulating the activity of Phosphodiesterase 6C, possibly through the activation of downstream signaling pathways. The observed scores indicate a consistent and plausible relationship, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the exact mechanism and therapeutic potential.",
